$$SYNTAX-LEVEL 2
*(maize-genomic-nomenclature
  <b>Genome assembly names</b><br>
  Genome assemblies have both names and identifiers. The names are intended to bee
  human-readable, and look like:<br>
  <span style="margin-left:20px">[species id] - [cultivar] - [project] - [assembly version]</span><br>
  For example:
  <span style="margin-left:20px">Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0</span>
  <br><br>
  <b>Genome assembly identifiers</b><br>
  Assemblies are also assigned an identifier, which is used to identify which genome, 
  and which assembly version a gene model belongs to. An assembly identifier looks like:<br>
  <span style="margin-left:20px">[species id][5 digits][version]</span><br>
  For example:
  Zm00001e = the 5th version of the B73 reference genome, where 'e' represents the 5th
  assembly version. This system will permit unique names for up to 99,999 genome 
  assemblies. It is impossible to predict how many genome assemblies will be produced, 
  so the naming scheme accomodates more than what seems a reasonable number.
  <br><br>
  <b>Gene model names</b><br>
  The assembly identifier and annotation version are used as prefixes for gene models.
  For example, the current set official B73 gene models belong to the second annotation 
  of the version 5 assembly, so the gene model prefix is Zm00001eb, where b indicates 
  the second annotation. Gene models in B73v5, second annotation look like:<br>
  <span style="margin-left:20px">Zm00001eb001020</span><br>
  This naming scheme ensures that each gene model has a unique identifier, and indicates
  which genome, assembly, and annotation version it belongs to.
  <br><br>
) 
